00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:50.900
news is absorbed. With the options income theme fresh in mind, let's cover the basics every Canadian

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:58.280
investor should know. Imagine you own 100 shares of a Canadian bank trading at $65 that just raised

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:04.600
its dividend. You want extra yield without selling. Selling a covered call at the $68 strike for a

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:13.360
$1.20 premium gives you 1.85% income over 30 days on top of the dividend but caps upside if the stock

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:19.540
rallies sharply. The mechanism works because the premium reflects both intrinsic value which is zero

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:26.060
here and time value driven by volatility and days to expiration. Professionals always check

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:33.380
open interest and bid ask with before entering. A wide spread on low volume strikes can erase half the

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:39.460
theoretical yield through slippage. The biggest mistake retail investors make is treating covered calls

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:45.520
as free money without realizing they are short a call that can be assigned on news. Instead,

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:51.400
always size the position so the premium covers at least 60% of your expected downside over the

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:56.940
holding period. While you're in your brokerage looking at those option chains, here are two new

